The use of commercial off-the-shelf (COTS) parts has become increasingly
widespread in the space sector, driven by the lower cost and higher availability
of components addressing large terrestrial markets. This trend, accelerated by
the NewSpace era and the rise of CubeSats, stands in contrast to the harsh and
unforgiving space environment. This thesis provides an overview of satellite
systems and their applications across the different Earth orbits, and examines
the environmental factors, vacuum, launch loads and radiation, that must be
considered when qualifying COTS parts for spaceflight. Two effects are analysed
in more detail: atmospheric drag on satellites in low earth orbit and its use for
propellant-free orbit control, and radiation exposure on astronauts together with
passive and active mitigation strategies.

